The `super` keyword lets a subclass reach into its parent — its fields, its methods, and its constructor. There are three distinct uses, and all three come up in interviews.

## 1. super to access a shadowed field

When a child class declares a field with the same name as the parent's, the parent's version is hidden — not gone. `super.fieldName` reads the parent's version directly.

```java
class Vehicle { int maxSpeed = 120; }
class Car extends Vehicle {
    int maxSpeed = 180;   // shadows parent field
    void display() {
        System.out.println(super.maxSpeed); // 120 — Vehicle's version
        System.out.println(this.maxSpeed);  // 180 — Car's version
    }
}
```

Field shadowing is generally a design smell — two identically named fields in a hierarchy cause confusion. But interviewers probe it to check whether you understand field hiding (static, reference-type-based) vs method overriding (dynamic, object-type-based).

## 2. super.method() to extend parent behavior

When a child overrides a method, `super.method()` calls the parent's version. Use this when you want to extend the parent's behavior — add to it — rather than replace it entirely.

```java
class GuideDog extends Dog {
    @Override
    public String describe() {
        return super.describe() + " [trained guide dog]"; // adds, doesn't replace
    }
}
```

This is common in the template method pattern, where the parent defines the structure and the child fills in specific steps.

## 3. super() for constructor chaining

When a subclass object is created, Java runs the entire constructor chain — from the subclass up through every ancestor to `Object`. If your constructor doesn't explicitly call `super(args)`, the compiler inserts `super()` — the no-arg version — as the first statement. If the parent has no no-arg constructor, you get a compile error on a line you didn't write.

`super(args)` must always be the **first statement** in a constructor. You cannot initialize child fields before calling super.

```java
class Animal {
    private String name;
    // Defining a parameterized constructor means the compiler
    // no longer generates a no-arg one automatically
    public Animal(String name) { this.name = name; }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    private String breed;

    public Dog(String breed) {
        // Compiler silently inserts: super()  ← no-arg version
        // Animal has no no-arg constructor
        // Compile error: "constructor Animal() is undefined"
        this.breed = breed;
    }
}
```

The confusion: developers look at Dog's constructor, it looks fine syntactically. The error points to the first line of the constructor body — because that's where the invisible `super()` was inserted.

```java
class Dog extends Animal {
    private String breed;

    public Dog(String name, String breed) {
        super(name);        // MUST be first — initializes Animal's fields
        this.breed = breed; // then Dog's own initialization
    }
}

// Subtle trap: parent constructor calling an overridden method
class Animal {
    Animal() { init(); } // runs before any child constructor body
    void init() {}
}
class Dog extends Animal {
    private String label = "Dog"; // not initialized yet when init() runs
    @Override
    void init() {
        System.out.println(label); // prints null — Dog constructor hasn't run
    }
}
```

The field `label` is `null` because the parent constructor finishes before the child's constructor body executes — before any field initializers in the child. The JVM called `Dog.init()` via vtable dispatch, but Dog's fields are still at their zero-defaults.

Intuitively, a square is a rectangle — same four sides, just equal. So `Square extends Rectangle` seems like a natural IS-A. The problem appears when any code uses what `Rectangle` actually promises.

```java
class Rectangle {
    protected int width, height;
    public void setWidth(int w)  { this.width = w; }
    public void setHeight(int h) { this.height = h; }
    public int area() { return width * height; }
}

class Square extends Rectangle {
    // Must keep width == height — override both setters
    @Override public void setWidth(int w)  { this.width = this.height = w; }
    @Override public void setHeight(int h) { this.width = this.height = h; }
}

// Any caller working with Rectangle expects independent dimensions:
void resize(Rectangle r) {
    r.setWidth(4);
    r.setHeight(5);
    assert r.area() == 20; // fails with Square: 5 × 5 = 25
}
```

A `Rectangle` caller has a reasonable assumption: setting width doesn't change height. Square breaks that assumption silently. This is the **Liskov Substitution Principle**: if a subclass cannot fully honor the parent's behavioral contract, the inheritance is wrong — even if the IS-A name sounds correct.

The right design: `Square` and `Rectangle` both extend a `Shape` base that declares `area()` without promising independent dimensions. The hierarchy fits the actual relationship, not the mathematical definition.

The most practical use of `this()` is the **telescoping constructor pattern** — multiple constructors with different parameter counts, where each shorter one delegates to the next longer one. The fully-parameterized constructor is the only one that actually does the work.

Without this pattern, you'd duplicate initialization logic in every constructor. Change a default value or add validation, and you'd need to update three places. The telescoping pattern funnels everything through one constructor that handles all the logic.

```java
public class Connection {
    private final String host;
    private final int port;
    private final int timeout;

    public Connection() {
        this("localhost", 8080); // delegates to 2-arg
    }

    public Connection(String host, int port) {
        this(host, port, 30_000); // delegates to 3-arg
    }

    public Connection(String host, int port, int timeout) {
        super(); // implicit if omitted — calls Object()
        this.host = host;
        this.port = port;
        this.timeout = timeout; // all validation lives here
    }
}
```

Only the three-argument constructor calls `super()` and assigns fields. The other two are convenience entry points. If you later add a `maxRetries` parameter, you add it to the full constructor and create a new delegating one — existing callers don't break. This is the same pattern that builders eventually replace when the parameter count exceeds four or five.

Without `@Override`, a typo in the method name silently creates a new method instead of overriding the parent's. Consider spelling `speak()` as `speek()` — without the annotation, the compiler accepts it as a valid new method. With `@Override`, it fails at compile time because no parent method matches.

```java
class Animal {
    String speak() { return "..."; }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    String speek() { return "Woof!"; } // COMPILE ERROR: no method to override
}
```

This is particularly dangerous with **covariant return types** and parameter type subtleties. A method that takes `Object` vs one that takes `String` creates an overload, not an override — without `@Override`, you'd never know your "override" is actually a separate method that the JVM will never dispatch to polymorphically. The annotation costs nothing and catches these errors immediately.

Method overloading is resolved entirely at compile time. The compiler looks at the method name and the types of the arguments at the call site, then picks the best matching method. This happens before the program runs — before any objects are created.

The key subtlety: the compiler uses the **declared type** of the argument, not the actual runtime type. If you have a variable typed as `Animal` and pass it to an overloaded method, the compiler resolves to the `Animal` version — even if the object at runtime is actually a `Dog`. This is the reference-type trap that interviewers probe:

```java
void describe(Animal a) { System.out.println("Animal"); }
void describe(Dog d)    { System.out.println("Dog"); }

Animal a = new Dog();
describe(a); // prints "Animal" — compiler sees Animal, locks in at compile time
```

Overloading is a **convenience feature**, not polymorphism in the architectural sense. It lets you use the same method name for related operations (`calculate(int)`, `calculate(double)`) without renaming them. The JVM doesn't participate — by the time the bytecode is generated, the call is resolved to a specific method signature.

```java
// Breaks abstraction — callers depend on ArrayList, not the List contract
ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();

// Later: swap to unmodifiable? Must change this line AND every caller
// Later: swap to LinkedList for insert performance? Same problem
// One concrete class → every change ripples everywhere
```

```java
// Abstracted — callers never know or care what implementation runs
List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
List<String> names = new LinkedList<>();             // swap: zero callers affected
List<String> names = Collections.unmodifiableList(src); // or this

// Your own code — same principle:
interface UserRepository {
    User findById(long id);
    void save(User user);
}

class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repo; // depends on interface, not PostgresUserRepository

    public UserService(UserRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }
    // Swap PostgresUserRepository for FakeUserRepository in tests: UserService never changes
}
```

The diamond problem arises when a class implements two interfaces that both provide a `default` method with the same signature. Java doesn't guess — it forces the implementing class to override and explicitly choose which version to use.

```java
public interface Loggable {
    default void log(String msg) {
        System.out.println("[LOG] " + msg);
    }
}

public interface Auditable {
    default void log(String msg) {
        System.out.println("[AUDIT] " + msg);
    }
}

public class OrderService implements Loggable, Auditable {
    @Override
    public void log(String msg) {
        Loggable.super.log(msg); // explicitly choose Loggable's version
    }
}
```

The resolution follows **three rules in priority order**: (1) a concrete class method always wins over any interface default, (2) a more specific sub-interface wins over its parent interface, and (3) if it's still ambiguous, the implementing class must override or the code won't compile. The syntax `InterfaceName.super.method()` lets you delegate to a specific interface's default when you need to reuse its logic rather than writing from scratch.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    class A {
        +greet() "default"
    }
    class B {
        +greet() "default from B"
    }
    class C {
        +greet() "default from C"
    }
    class D
    A <|-- B : extends
    A <|-- C : extends
    B <|.. D : implements
    C <|.. D : implements
```

If `A`, `B`, `C` are interfaces and `B`/`C` both provide a `default greet()`, calling `d.greet()` is ambiguous — the compiler forces `D` to override `greet()` and explicitly choose (`B.super.greet()`, `C.super.greet()`, or a merged body).

```java
interface Animal {
    default String greet() { return "Hello"; }
}

interface Friendly extends Animal {
    @Override
    default String greet() { return "Friendly Hello"; }
}

interface Polite extends Animal {
    @Override
    default String greet() { return "Polite Hello"; }
}

// Both Friendly and Polite override greet() — diamond is real
class Dog implements Friendly, Polite {
    // COMPILE ERROR: class Dog inherits unrelated defaults for greet()
    // from types Friendly and Polite
}
```

The compiler refuses to pick — it requires `Dog` to override `greet()` itself.

```java
class Dog implements Friendly, Polite {
    @Override
    public String greet() {
        // pick one:
        return Friendly.super.greet();
        // or combine them:
        // return Friendly.super.greet() + " + " + Polite.super.greet();
    }
}
```

The `Interface.super.method()` syntax — introduced specifically for this case — lets you reach past the ambiguity to a specific parent interface's `default` body. This keeps the resolution explicit in the code rather than governed by inheritance order.

When Java looks up which method to run for `obj.method()`, it follows three rules in order:

**Rule 1 — Class always wins.** If the class itself (or any superclass) provides an implementation, the interface `default` is ignored. This is why you rarely see diamonds in practice — adding a concrete method in the class makes the ambiguity disappear.
```java
class Dog implements Friendly, Polite {
    @Override
    public String greet() { return "Woof"; } // class wins over both interface defaults
}
```

**Rule 2 — More specific interface wins.** If one interface `extends` the other, the child interface's default wins because it's closer in the hierarchy.
```java
interface A { default String hi() { return "A"; } }
interface B extends A { default String hi() { return "B"; } }
class C implements A, B {} // B's hi() wins — no ambiguity
```

**Rule 3 — Otherwise, explicit override is required.** If neither of the above applies (two unrelated interfaces both provide `default`), the class **must override** and usually delegates via `Interface.super.method()`. The compiler throws a clear error if you don't, which means diamond bugs are caught at compile time, not production.

Inheritance still makes sense when three things are true simultaneously: the relationship is genuinely IS-A (a `Dog` IS-A `Animal`), the child is a true specialization of the parent rather than just sharing some code, and you don't foresee needing to swap or combine behaviors at runtime. The template method pattern — where an abstract parent defines an algorithm skeleton and children override specific steps — is the strongest remaining use case for inheritance.

The refactoring pattern that replaces most inheritance looks like this. Instead of a `ReportGenerator` base class with `PdfReportGenerator` and `CsvReportGenerator` children, you create a `ReportGenerator` that holds a `ReportFormatter` interface as a field. The formatting strategy is injected, making `PdfReportGenerator` a configuration choice rather than a class hierarchy.

```java
public class ReportGenerator {
    private final ReportFormatter formatter; // composed, not inherited
    private final DataFetcher fetcher;

    public ReportGenerator(ReportFormatter formatter, DataFetcher fetcher) {
        this.formatter = formatter;
        this.fetcher = fetcher;
    }

    public byte[] generate(ReportRequest request) {
        var data = fetcher.fetch(request);
        return formatter.format(data); // behavior swappable at runtime
    }
}
```

With this design, adding an Excel export doesn't require a new subclass — you implement `ReportFormatter` and inject it. The `ReportGenerator` class never changes, which means existing tests keep passing. That's the practical win composition gives you: **open for extension, closed for modification**, without inheritance's coupling cost.

Composition is the right choice when the part has no meaningful existence outside the whole. A `Room` doesn't exist outside a `House`. An `OrderLine` doesn't exist outside an `Order`. In Java, this means the owner calls `new` inside its own constructor — there's no external constructor parameter for the part, no setter, and no way to share it with another container.

```java
public class Car {
    private final Engine engine; // composition — Car owns the lifecycle

    public Car() {
        this.engine = new Engine(); // created here, garbage-collected with Car
    }
    // No setter, no way to inject a different engine — intentional
}
```

When `Car` goes out of scope and is garbage-collected, `Engine` goes with it — because no external reference exists. In a database schema, this maps to `CASCADE DELETE`: delete the order, delete all its order lines.

Aggregation is the right choice when the part exists independently of any particular owner and might be shared across multiple owners. A `Department` has professors, but professors exist before any department and continue to exist after. In Java, the part is passed in via constructor or setter — it's injected, not created.

```java
public class Department {
    private final List<Professor> professors; // aggregation — passed from outside

    public Department(List<Professor> professors) {
        this.professors = professors; // professors outlive this department
    }
}
```

When `Department` is garbage-collected, `Professor` objects survive — other references to them exist. In a database schema, this maps to no cascade: delete the department, professors stay. Spring's `@Autowired` dependency injection is aggregation — the bean lives in the container independently and can be shared by any number of classes.

The failure mode is specific and worth tracing. Consider a `Person` class that overrides `equals()` to compare by name and age, but inherits the default `hashCode()` from `Object`.

```java
Person p1 = new Person("Alice", 30);
Person p2 = new Person("Alice", 30);

p1.equals(p2); // true — same name and age
p1.hashCode() == p2.hashCode(); // false — different object addresses

Map<Person, String> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put(p1, "engineer");
map.get(p2); // returns null — p2 hashes to a different bucket
```

`put()` stores the entry in the bucket determined by `p1.hashCode()`. `get(p2)` computes `p2.hashCode()`, which returns a different number because it's based on the memory address of a different object. The `get()` call looks in the wrong bucket and never finds the entry. **No exception is thrown** — the map simply behaves as if the entry doesn't exist.

The fix is straightforward: override both methods together, using the same fields in both. `Objects.hash(name, age)` for `hashCode()` and field-by-field comparison for `equals()`. Or use a `record` — `record Person(String name, int age) {}` generates both correctly from all fields, and the implementations are consistent by construction.

```java
class User {
    private final String email;
    User(String email) { this.email = email; }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof User u)) return false;
        return email.equals(u.email);
    }
    // NOTE: no hashCode() override — inherits Object.hashCode() (identity-based)
}

Map<User, String> cache = new HashMap<>();
cache.put(new User("a@b.com"), "Alice");
String name = cache.get(new User("a@b.com")); // returns null — bug!
```

Why: the two `User` instances are **equal** (same email) but their `Object.hashCode()` returns different identity-based values. `put()` stored under hash 12345; `get()` searches hash 98765 — wrong bucket, no entry found, returns null. Every `HashMap` operation on this class is broken.

```java
import java.util.Objects;

class User {
    private final String email;
    private final String name;

    User(String email, String name) { this.email = email; this.name = name; }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof User u)) return false;
        return Objects.equals(email, u.email) && Objects.equals(name, u.name);
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(email, name); // must include the same fields as equals
    }
}
```

**Key constraints:**
- Include the **same fields** in both methods — mismatch breaks the contract.
- Use **immutable fields** (`final`) — if a field used in the hash mutates while the object is a map key, the object becomes unreachable.
- Or use a **Java 14+ `record`**, which generates both methods automatically: `record User(String email, String name) {}`.

Java gives you several mechanisms, each with different trade-offs. The **copy constructor** is the most practical for most cases — it's explicit, type-safe, and lets you decide exactly which fields need deep copying.

```java
public class Order {
    private final String id;
    private final List<Item> items;
    private final LocalDate created;

    // Copy constructor — deep copies only the mutable field
    public Order(Order original) {
        this.id = original.id;           // String is immutable — safe to share
        this.items = new ArrayList<>(original.items); // new list, same Item refs
        this.created = original.created; // LocalDate is immutable — safe to share
    }
}
```

Notice that `new ArrayList<>(original.items)` creates a new list but the `Item` objects inside are still shared. If `Item` is mutable, you need to copy those too — `original.items.stream().map(Item::new).toList()` where `Item` also has a copy constructor. This is why **deeply nested mutable graphs** are expensive to copy correctly.

For quick-and-dirty deep copying of complex graphs, serialization works — serialize to bytes and deserialize back. It's slow and requires `Serializable`, but it handles arbitrary nesting without manual copy logic. In practice, the best approach is designing with immutable objects from the start — if nothing can be mutated after construction, the shallow vs deep question becomes irrelevant.

Each requirement addresses a specific mutation vector. Miss any one, and the class is mutable.

**1. Make the class `final`** — prevents subclasses from adding mutable fields or overriding methods to introduce mutation. Without `final`, a subclass like `MutablePoint extends Point` could add setters.

**2. Make all fields `private final`** — `private` prevents direct access, `final` prevents reassignment after construction. Both are required — `final` without `private` allows reflection-based mutation, and `private` without `final` allows internal reassignment.

**3. No setter or mutation methods** — any method that modifies state makes the class mutable, even if fields are `final` (a `final List` can still be modified via `add()`).

**4. Defensive copies for mutable components** — this is the step most developers forget.

```java
public final class Event {
    private final String name;
    private final List<String> tags;

    public Event(String name, List<String> tags) {
        this.name = name;
        this.tags = List.copyOf(tags); // defensive copy in constructor
    }

    public List<String> getTags() {
        return tags; // safe — List.copyOf returns unmodifiable
    }
}
```

`List.copyOf()` creates an unmodifiable copy, so neither the caller's original list nor the accessor's return value can mutate the Event's internal state. Using `Collections.unmodifiableList(tags)` is insufficient — it wraps the original list, so modifications to the original still affect the wrapper.

The most practical consequence of `static` in nested classes is **memory leak prevention**. A non-static inner class holds a hidden `Outer.this` reference, meaning the outer object can never be garbage collected while the inner object is alive. A static nested class has no such reference.

```java
public class ConnectionPool {
    private static int totalConnections = 0;
    private final String url;

    ConnectionPool(String url) {
        this.url = url;
        totalConnections++;
    }

    static class Config {
        final int timeout;
        Config(int timeout) { this.timeout = timeout; }
    }

    class Monitor {
        void status() {
            System.out.println(url + ": " + totalConnections);
        }
    }
}
```

`static class Config` can be created without any `ConnectionPool` instance — `new ConnectionPool.Config(30)` works on its own. The inner `class Monitor` requires `pool.new Monitor()` because it needs the outer reference to access `url`. If you store `Monitor` in a cache or listener list, the `ConnectionPool` it references can never be collected. **Default to static nested classes** unless you genuinely need instance-level access to the outer object.

Static fields and static blocks execute in **textual order** when the class is first loaded — typically on first reference. This matters because:

1. A static field initialized with a method call runs that method during class loading, before any instance exists.
2. If class A's static block references class B, and class B's static block references class A, you get a **circular initialization** — one of them sees the other's fields as zero/null.
3. An exception thrown during static initialization wraps in `ExceptionInInitializerError` — and the class becomes permanently unusable in that classloader.

```java
class Config {
    // Executes top-to-bottom during class loading
    static final String ENV = System.getenv("APP_ENV");  // 1st
    static final int PORT;                                // 2nd (blank final)

    static {                                              // 3rd
        PORT = "prod".equals(ENV) ? 443 : 8080;
        // If System.getenv throws, ExceptionInInitializerError
        // and Config can never be used again in this JVM
    }
}
```

The practical rule: keep static initialization simple and side-effect-free. Heavy setup (database connections, HTTP clients) belongs in explicit `init()` methods or dependency injection, not in static blocks.

The most powerful pattern with enums is **constant-specific method implementation** — each constant overrides an abstract method defined in the enum, effectively implementing the Strategy pattern without any separate class hierarchy.

```java
public enum PricingTier {
    STANDARD {
        @Override
        public double applyDiscount(double price) {
            return price;
        }
    },
    PREMIUM {
        @Override
        public double applyDiscount(double price) {
            return price * 0.85; // 15% off
        }
    },
    ENTERPRISE {
        @Override
        public double applyDiscount(double price) {
            return price * 0.70; // 30% off
        }
    };

    public abstract double applyDiscount(double price);
}
```

Each constant carries its own behavior. Calling `tier.applyDiscount(100.0)` dispatches to the correct implementation without any `if-else` chain. **Adding a new tier forces you to implement the method** — the compiler won't let you create a constant without providing the abstract method body. This is the advantage over `switch` — a switch statement silently ignores new constants unless you add a `default` or the compiler warns you. An abstract method makes the requirement structural.

```java
class EventProcessor {
    private final String name = "processor-1";

    void register() {
        Runnable handler = new Runnable() {
            private int count = 0; // inner classes can have state

            @Override
            public void run() {
                count++;
                System.out.println(this.count);  // this = anon instance
                System.out.println(this.getClass().getName()); // EventProcessor$1
                // To access outer fields:
                System.out.println(EventProcessor.this.name); // explicit outer reference
            }
        };
    }
}
```

The anonymous class has its own `this`. To reach the enclosing instance you use **`EnclosingClass.this.field`**. It also holds an implicit reference to the enclosing `EventProcessor` — which is why keeping a long-lived anonymous listener inside a short-lived outer instance is the classic Android/Swing **memory leak**.

```java
class EventProcessor {
    private final String name = "processor-1";

    void register() {
        Runnable handler = () -> {
            System.out.println(this);               // this = EventProcessor instance
            System.out.println(this.name);          // no Outer.this ceremony
            System.out.println(this.getClass());    // EventProcessor, not a synthetic class
        };
    }
}
```

Inside a lambda, `this` is just the enclosing class's `this`. The compiler doesn't create a new class — there's no separate inner instance to refer to. This also means lambdas **can't have their own fields**: they capture variables from the enclosing scope, but those variables must be `final` or **effectively final** (never reassigned).
